Here is a complete below-the-boundary example.
```javascript
const temperature = 17;
const limit = 19;
const below = temperature < limit;
const atLimit = temperature === limit;
const above = temperature > limit;
console.log(below, atLimit, above);
```
The first two lines supply numbers. Each following declaration stores a Boolean. The output is `true false false`: 17 is below 19, is not equal to 19, and is not above 19. Commas separate the three values passed into `console.log()`.

A boundary is the exact point where a comparison changes. Being exactly at the limit is not being above it. This second example makes that distinction visible.
```javascript
const temperature = 19;
const limit = 19;
console.log(temperature < limit);
console.log(temperature === limit);
console.log(temperature > limit);
console.log(temperature >= limit);
```
The four output lines are false, true, false, true. The final sign includes the equal case, so 19 qualifies as greater than or equal to 19.

